---
import "@fontsource-variable/geist/wght.css";
import TypeSpecimen from "@/components/TypeSpecimen.astro";
import { getPublishedEntries } from "@/lib/content";
import { url } from "@/lib/paths";

const entries = await getPublishedEntries();
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "the-first-version-is-only-a-beginning", text: "The first version is only a beginning" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "clarity-is-a-maintenance-feature", text: "Clarity is a maintenance feature" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "state-belongs-in-one-place", text: "State belongs in one place" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "typography-for-product-surfaces", text: "Typography for product surfaces" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "what-to-look-for", text: "What to look for" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "compare-the-typefaces", text: "Compare the typefaces" },
];
---

<TypeSpecimen
  pageTitle="Software That Ages Well — Geist Essay"
  description="An essay on durable software, clear state, and product typography, set in Geist with long-form reading samples."
  eyebrow="ESSAY / PRODUCT SANS"
  titleLines={["Software That", "Ages Well"]}
  lede="Durable software is shaped by decisions that remain understandable after the first release. This essay uses Geist at the same body size and spacing shown in the reference: 17px text, #141414 color, and 20px paragraph margins."
  glyphLines={[
    "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ",
    "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz",
    "0123456789 & @ # % ? ! / ( ) [ ]",
  ]}
  meta={["Geist Variable", "100-900", "Latin subset", "Self-hosted"]}
  fontClass="article-prose--geist"
  entries={entries}
  headings={headings}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-first-version-is-only-a-beginning">The first version is only a beginning</h2>
    <p>
      A product is easiest to understand on the day it is released. The team still remembers
      why each decision was made, which constraints shaped the architecture, and which parts
      were expected to change. A year later, that context lives in code, tests, interfaces,
      documentation, and the habits of the people who maintain it. The quality of those
      surfaces determines how gracefully the product can age.
    </p>
    <p>
      Durable software does not try to predict every future requirement. It creates clear
      boundaries so future changes have somewhere to land. A new provider can be added
      without rewriting the interface. A new state can be introduced without duplicating the
      source of truth. A visual change can move through shared tokens instead of becoming a
      collection of local exceptions.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why maintenance is a design concern rather than a cleanup phase. The shape of
      the system teaches the next engineer how to work inside it. When the shape is clear,
      changes become smaller in concept even when they touch several files. When the shape is
      unclear, every change begins with archaeology.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="clarity-is-a-maintenance-feature">Clarity is a maintenance feature</h2>
    <p>
      Code becomes expensive when a reader has to reconstruct state from several unrelated
      places. A component receives one value from a parent, another from context, and a third
      from a global store. The render looks simple, but the behavior depends on a hidden
      sequence of updates. The cost appears later, when a timing bug cannot be explained
      without tracing the entire application.
    </p>
    <p>
      Clear systems make ownership explicit. A piece of state belongs to one layer. A derived
      value is calculated from a named input. A side effect has one lifecycle. These choices
      reduce the number of states that can exist at the same time, which makes both testing
      and debugging more direct.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same idea applies to visual hierarchy. A heading has one role. A button has one
      primary treatment. A paragraph sits inside a predictable reading measure. The interface
      becomes easier to extend because new elements inherit an existing grammar instead of
      negotiating a new one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="state-belongs-in-one-place">State belongs in one place</h2>
    <p>
      State duplication is one of the most reliable sources of long-term complexity. The
      duplicate may begin as a convenience: a local boolean that mirrors a server response,
      or a derived label copied into another component. The copies remain harmless until one
      update path forgets to synchronize them.
    </p>
    <p>
      A better system gives every fact one owner and derives everything else. The owner may
      be a database, a controller, a component, or a URL. What matters is that the ownership
      boundary is visible. Once the boundary is clear, performance work has a target, loading
      states have a source, and tests can describe behavior without recreating the entire
      application.
    </p>
    <h3>Make the default state explicit</h3>
    <p>
      Defaults are part of the contract. A missing value should not silently become an empty
      string, a hidden loading state, or a different visual tone. Explicit defaults make the
      first render predictable and give future states a stable place to begin.
    </p>
    <h3>Keep transitions observable</h3>
    <p>
      A state change should leave evidence. The interface can show progress, the controller
      can expose a status, and the logs can name the operation. Observable transitions turn
      a race condition into a sequence that can be measured and corrected.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="typography-for-product-surfaces">Typography for product surfaces</h2>
    <p>
      Product typography has to work at several scales. A landing page needs a memorable
      display voice, a dashboard needs compact labels, and a long explanation needs a
      comfortable reading rhythm. Geist approaches those roles with a neutral, contemporary
      sans-serif structure. Its forms are direct, its spacing is even, and the variable weight
      range allows one family to carry both interface and editorial text.
    </p>
    <p>
      At 17px, the body size shown in the reference, Geist produces a measured reading pace.
      The color <strong>#141414</strong> keeps contrast high without using absolute black, and
      the 20px paragraph interval gives each block enough separation to scan without breaking
      the flow. These values create a surface that feels calm and precise at the same time.
    </p>
    <p>
      The typeface also supports a practical visual hierarchy. Medium weights can distinguish
      headings and controls, while regular text remains quiet enough for sustained reading.
      The family does not depend on decoration to create emphasis. Hierarchy comes from size,
      weight, spacing, and order.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="what-to-look-for">What to look for</h2>
    <p>
      Compare this article with the other specimens at the same browser width. Read a full
      paragraph before judging the face. At paragraph scale, the important qualities are the
      line endings, the amount of space inside words, and the speed at which the eye returns
      to the next line.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The 17px body size creates a compact but comfortable reading rhythm.</li>
      <li>The 20px paragraph spacing separates ideas without making the article feel fragmented.</li>
      <li>The variable weight range gives headings and interface labels one shared type family.</li>
      <li>The neutral letterforms keep long technical explanations visually quiet.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="compare-the-typefaces">Compare the typefaces</h2>
    <p>
      Read the same kind of material across the specimen pages and notice how each typeface
      changes the pace without changing the content. The comparison becomes most useful at
      paragraph scale rather than in a single display word.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href={url("/type-specimens/dm-sans/")}>DM Sans</a> offers a warm humanist voice with compact proportional spacing.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/type-specimens/ibm-plex-mono/")}>IBM Plex Mono</a> brings a fixed technical grid with a softer editorial finish.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/weekly/2026-w44/")}>Manrope</a> provides a geometric rhythm for long-form product writing.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/weekly/2026-w43/")}>JetBrains Mono</a> emphasizes precision, character separation, and code-oriented structure.</li>
      <li>This page: Geist balances a product-interface voice with comfortable long-form reading.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</TypeSpecimen>
